one of the claims made by Prince Harry  in his book is that he killed 25 Taliban  fighters while he served in Afghanistan  but also claimed that he saw them not as  people but as chess pieces instead  well joining me now to discuss this  further is retired British Army Officer  Colonel Richard Kemp who commanded the  British forces in Afghanistan in 2003.  thanks so much for talking to us at  Colonel Kemp so in Prince Harry's Memoir  he talks about his time in Afghanistan  and says that he killed 25 Taliban  fighters as former commander of British  forces in Afghanistan how do you feel  about those comments  I think they're probably ill charge  partly for two reasons really one is uh  his suggestion that he killed 25 will  have re-re incited I think those people  who wish him harm as a result of his  military service that was 10 years ago  now and the memories might have faded  well now they've been resurrected and  there will be people out there who maybe  support the Taliban uh support their  agenda who who will now be motivated I  think to to kill Harry well let's hope  they don't succeed and I'm sure he's got  pretty good security but that's one  problem the other problem I found with  his comments was that he characterized  the British army basically as having  trained him and other soldiers to see  his enemy as less than human just as  chess pieces on the board to be swiped  off which is not the case it's the  opposite of the case  um the British army is very careful to  ensure that its soldiers are trained to  respect their enemy particularly if  they're if they're uh if they're killed  and they their bodies need to be treated  with respect and if they're wounded then  they have to be given medical treatment  and if they are taken prisoner they have  to be treated according to Junior  conventions so it's not the way he  describes it I think that's a very bad  judgment  so you suggest there could be an  increased security threat against a  Prince Harry himself could that extend  to any other serviceman who served in  Afghanistan do you think  yeah I think it could do because I I  wouldn't overemphasize this point  because of course those people who want  to kill British soldiers or former  soldiers they know that the British army  killed many more than 25 but it brings  it into kind of a Sharp focus and  particularly his comments about about  how they don't regard you know their  enemy Fighters as being human beings  even so I think it will this world  inside some people to to maybe attempt  to attack against British soldiers  anywhere in the world or former British  soldiers I like I said I wouldn't  overplay that aspect but I think the  impact on his own personal security is  greater but I don't think we can ignore  that  is it unusual for a member of the  British forces to talk openly about how  many people they've killed in war  I think sometimes they will do so uh  privately probably you know with their  own comrades  um and that's that's not uncommon it's  it is not very common particularly for  people to give exact numbers of this of  the enemy they had killed obviously in  in most cases soldiers on the ground  don't know how many people have killed  because a lot of these engagements take  place at long range Harry's in a  different position with the technology  available to him and of course there  have been other other former soldiers  who have written books about their  experience in Afghanistan which they've  said who they've killed but but not with  the kind of a profile that Harry has and  I think it's you know it's not it's not  there's nothing illegal about it it  doesn't go against any military  regulations or anything but I think it's  probably this particular uh Revelation I  don't think is in very good taste  do you think that these comments  undermine the positive work that Prince  Harry did while he was in the army  yeah Prince Harry was a very courageous  Soldier he did two operational tours in  Northern in Afghanistan  um he didn't have to serve in  Afghanistan in fact he had to fight  government policy that tried to prevent  him from going because of the threat to  the third in line to the throne as he  was then  um so he had a very good reputation  because of that he was highly regarded  and that reputation was enhanced by his  championing of wounded Veterans for  example in the Invictus games but I  think some veterans will that his his  achievements and his reputation won't be  destroyed but I think this will tarnish  it to some extent in the eyes of some  veterans and as I say but not so much  the number he killed but particularly  his characterization of the British army  as being an organization that treats  their enemy as as sub-human  as a member of the royal family he was  always going to be a very high profile  member of the British forces wasn't he  would he have had special advice from  the military on how to handle that  yeah I think so I think he he  undoubtedly would have done I don't know  for sure but I I'd be surprised if he  hadn't  um and you know particularly as I I  don't know but but normally if you're  going to publish a book that discusses  military operations you usually have to  get approval from excuse me approval  from the ministry of Defense to publish  it to ensure that nothing that's secret  is revealed and whether he did that in  this case or not I don't know but if he  did then I suspect it it passed the mod  censorship procedures uh because I don't  think anything that I've seen that he  said in the book is um is is damaging I  think what this seems to me the comments  he's made I think look to me kind of  their their fairly provocative  deliberately I think  um no doubt written by a Ghostwriter to  to get these books selling  um and that's you know that's a pretty  standard stuff when you're writing A  Memoir of this sort but I think he's  should have had the judgment to to  change the words of those that ghost  writing  on Sky News now having to comment on it  um not not particularly surprised I  think we um most of us saw this coming  in the vast number of kind of previews  we've had of of the fact that he's doing  documentaries and writing Memoirs Etc so  it doesn't overly surprise me but but I  think I'm somewhat disappointed because  I do think as I said that this has  tarnished his own reputation and it also  has damaged uh if people believe it and  many will believe it it has damaged the  reputation of the British army unjustly  because the the characterization that he  makes in this book is simply not true  it's it's it's in fact the opposite of  the truth  well Colonel Richard Kemp we really  appreciate your time this morning thanks  so much for your time and your response  to Prince Harry Harry's and Memoir  Revelations we appreciate it thank you
 

